            tagline = "A class project turns a forgotten neighbourhood map into a practical guide to community support.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "A Map with No Legend", listOf(
                    "The geography class received a large map of their neighbourhood from the school archive. It showed streets, public buildings, and dozens of coloured dots, but the legend had been lost. Teacher Mrs Ibe asked the students to investigate the map's purpose before adding anything to it. Tarela noticed that the dots clustered around places people visited every day.",
                    "The paper was dated twenty years earlier. A note on the back read COMMUNITY HELP POINTS—UPDATE ANNUALLY. The class wondered whether the dots marked public services, volunteer groups, or something else. They agreed to compare the map with current public information and not assume that an old location was still operating."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The First Blue Dot", listOf(
                    "The first blue dot sat beside the old bus depot, now a transport information office. Staff there remembered a volunteer desk that once helped new residents find local services. They no longer offered that service, but they kept a printed directory from the project. The directory listed libraries, public clinics, community centres, and youth activities.",
                    "The students copied only public contact details and checked each organisation's current information through official sources. Some entries had changed names, and two locations had closed. Tarela suggested marking outdated entries in a separate research layer rather than erasing them. The teacher approved: the map should show history and current information without confusing the two."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "The Green Dots", listOf(
                    "The green dots led to public gardens and reading corners. A retired volunteer, Mr Nkem, remembered that the map had been created by residents who wanted newcomers to know where they could find practical help. The dots did not represent charity alone; they included places to learn, rest, read, and meet neighbours.",
                    "Mr Nkem had a folder of meeting notes, but some pages contained personal names and phone numbers. The teacher explained that those details should not be copied into a public school project. The students used the public descriptions and asked the archive to preserve the private pages under its normal access rules."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "What Counts as Help", listOf(
                    "The class debated whether a sports club, library, public garden, or advice centre belonged on a map of support. Tarela argued that people needed more than emergency services; they also needed places where they could build skills and feel included. Her classmate Dayo said the map should avoid labelling people as needy.",
                    "They interviewed staff at several public venues using questions approved by the teacher. Each place described what it offered, who could attend, and whether visitors needed to register. The class created neutral categories: learning, recreation, public information, and community connection. They did not collect personal stories or ask visitors to explain why they used a service."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Missing Red Dot", listOf(
                    "One red dot appeared on a vacant building near the market. The archive notes called it the Welcome Room, but nobody in the class knew what it had been. The property now belonged to the council, and its public record described it as awaiting redevelopment. The students asked the local history office for more information.",
                    "A newspaper archive showed that the Welcome Room had once hosted language classes and practical workshops. It closed when the building needed repairs. The class found no evidence that it was currently available for public use, so they marked it as a historical site rather than an active service. Tarela said the map should never send someone to a locked door."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Current Directory", listOf(
                    "The students designed a digital directory with a date beside every listing. Each organisation received a draft entry to check its hours, accessibility information, and contact method. Several asked for corrections. The public library added its quiet study hours, while the community centre clarified that some activities required advance registration.",
                    "The class included a clear note that the directory was not an emergency service and that details could change. Mrs Ibe asked them to include a way to report outdated information without publishing private data. They used the school's official email address as the contact point, with a staff member responsible for reviewing submissions."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Neighbourhood Walk", listOf(
                    "With permission and adult supervision, the class walked a short public route to verify signs and entrances. They did not enter private property or photograph visitors. At one location, a ramp was temporarily blocked by maintenance work. The students recorded that access should be confirmed with staff before a visit, rather than marking the venue permanently inaccessible.",
                    "At the next meeting, the venue manager explained that the ramp would be clear after the scheduled work. The class updated the listing with a date and a reminder to check current access information. Dayo suggested including public transport stops near each venue. The geography teacher helped them use official route information and avoid estimating journey times."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "A Map People Can Read", listOf(
                    "The first draft used too many colours, so students with different visual needs found it difficult to follow. The class simplified the palette, added symbols with text labels, and created a large-print version. They also made a paper copy for the library. Tarela checked the legend with people who had not worked on the project.",
                    "The feedback revealed that the map needed clearer distinctions between active venues, historical sites, and places requiring registration. The students added those labels and a last-checked date. Mrs Ibe praised them for treating accessibility as part of the design rather than an afterthought."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Community Review", listOf(
                    "Representatives from the listed organisations met at the school to review the directory. They corrected a phone number, asked for one outdated listing to be removed, and suggested adding a public homework club. The class verified the club's schedule before including it. The archive confirmed that the original map could be displayed beside the updated version.",
                    "Mr Nkem attended and recognised the original colour pattern. He was pleased that the students had kept the history visible while checking every active listing. He reminded them that the map was never meant to rank places or people. It was a practical invitation to discover what the neighbourhood already offered."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "A Map That Keeps Moving", listOf(
                    "The library displayed both maps with a note explaining their dates and purposes. The digital directory included a review month, and the school assigned a new class to check it each term. Tarela volunteered to show the next group how to verify entries and protect private information.",
                    "The old map had captured a moment when residents tried to make their neighbourhood easier to navigate. The new one did the same for a different generation. Its most important feature was not the coloured dots but the habit behind them: ask, verify, update, and make information welcoming. The map of kindness was never finished, because a helpful community keeps learning how to guide one another."
                ))
            )
        )
